// ── Enrolment-code issuance: one root, modeled reads, a verdict bound to the running server ──
// EVERY fx_* READING BELOW IS REAL unless its claim says SYNTHETIC. Each FxRead is the exit status,
// stdout and stderr of one of the eighteen reads approval_ntfy_read_command pins (the pin claims
// below assert each argv), run under the same `timeout --kill-after 2s 10s` against the upstream
// ntfy v2.28.0 linux/arm64 release binary serving 127.0.0.1:2586 from a scratch config and auth
// database, once per scenario, with the deviation produced on the real server: a flag, an
// NTFY_* variable, another executable path, a config key, a config touched after start, the binary
// touched after start, the auth database renamed and replaced under the running server, the config
// made unreadable under it, a metrics listener, a restart between the first and last reads.
//
// WHAT IS NOT THE PINNED ARGV, and only this: the capture host runs no systemd, sudo, or tailscaled,
// so (a) the two unit reads and the manager environment are stubs answering the live server's PID
// and the environment it was really launched with; (b) the four `sudo -n -u gunbc-ntfy` reads ran
// their inner argv as the server's own uid, and `id -u` ran without its operand (the answer is the
// server's real uid); (c) the tailscale serve status is the documented JSON shape (as in
// test.claim.tailscale_serve_status), not a capture. The fixtures then rewrite scratch paths to the
// declared ones, keep only the btime line of /proc/stat, and keep only the header and LISTEN rows of
// /proc/net/tcp and tcp6, dropping LISTEN rows of OTHER processes that share the capture uid (a
// terminal server on 7681); on srv1 the server's principal is dedicated to it. The listing_* rows
// are real ntfy access stdout from the same binary, placed in the exact reading's access read.
type FxRead {
  read: String
  exit_code: Int
  stdout: String
  stderr: String
}

